review_comment:
> That value was placed just to be a placeholder for the default format.

I understand. The thing is that any other non-`None` value will also lead to the default format, so `"standard"` is not particularly a placeholder. For example, the docs say that the argument "may either be the special string `'iso-8601'` [...] or the special string `'standard'`, which indicates that Django interval format", while in fact *any* string except `iso-8601` indicates Django interval format.

It seems like it would be best if not defining the setting leads to the old behavior, and defining the setting with the ISO 8601 value leads to the corresponding behavior - no other default placeholder etc. So, why can't we simply have this minimal change?
```python
    def to_representation(self, value):
        output_format = getattr(self, 'format', api_settings.DURATION_FORMAT)

        if output_format.lower() == ISO_8601:
            return duration_iso_string(value)

        return duration_string(value)
```

I realize that this does not allow returning a string (if the value already is one), but AFAICT that wasn't the point of this PR in the first place (it's not mentioned in the feature request, #8527). If really needed, we could cover this case by checking for `output_format == 'none'`. In any case, this would get rid of the meaningless `'standard'` magic value.

Also, please don't forget testing what happens if an undefined format is passed.

