review_comment:
@adrinjalali Removing `_get_tags` is a breaking change if developers are using it. Looking through Github for [self._get_tags(](https://github.com/search?q=self._get_tags&type=code), there are some scikit-learn related use cases for `_get_tags` but it's not too much.

@glemaitre Imbalanced-learn uses `_get_tags` in tests: https://github.com/scikit-learn-contrib/imbalanced-learn/blob/2b6269f9aaea5f058606bf318b8bc36150137dd6/imblearn/utils/estimator_checks.py#L101

Given this is a private API, I am okay with breaking it.

On top of the slice fix above, guard against non-slice values so this can't silently regress. Validating here at read-time (rather than at the use-site in `test_setting_request_on_sub_estimator_removes_error`) is important: the use-site only looks up `method_name`s in `estimator_routing_methods`, so the bad RFECV `predict`/`score` entries would never be checked there. Read-time validation catches misconfig regardless of whether it's exercised.

```suggestion
    if "filter_registry" in metaestimator_info:
        filter_registry = metaestimator_info["filter_registry"]
        for method_name, index in filter_registry.items():
            if not isinstance(index, slice):
                raise TypeError(
                    f"`filter_registry` values must be slices, got {index!r} for "
                    f"method {method_name!r}. Use e.g. `slice(-1, None)` to select "
                    "the last fitted sub-estimator."
                )
```

Verified locally: with this guard but before the slice fix, the RFECV case raises `TypeError`; after the slice fix, the full suite passes.

The comment above might help too but I think maybe we can split this to make it much cleaner.

```suggestion
    for i, frame in enumerate(stack):
        if frame in METHODS + ["consuming_metric"]:
            callee = frame
            break

    for frame in stack[i + 1:]:
        if frame in METHODS + validation_functions:
            caller = frame
```

