I found the removing and adding again of the remainder a bit hard to understand. Here is an alternative suggestion for the `if` condition that follows the approach from the `else` branch below, but feel free to ignore if you don't like it.
```python
if hasattr(self, "transformers_"):
    if self.transformers_[-1][0] == "remainder":
        remainder_columns = self._remainder[2]
        # Remove remainder if "drop" or no remainder columns to display
        if not remainder_columns or self.remainder == "drop":
            transformers = self.transformers_[:-1]
        else:
            # Reformat remainder because "passthrough" is converted to
            # FunctionTransformer internally when fitted
            transformers = chain(
            self.transformers_[:-1], [("remainder", self.remainder, self.transformers_[-1][2])]
            )
    else:
        transformers = self.transformers_
```

Which means the code can actually be reduced even more to:
```python

if hasattr(self, "transformers_"):
    if self.transformers_[-1][0] == "remainder":
        # Remove remainder if "drop"
        if self.remainder == "drop":
            transformers = self.transformers_[:-1]
        else:
            # Reformat remainder because "passthrough" is converted to
            # FunctionTransformer internally when fitted
            transformers = chain(
            self.transformers_[:-1], [("remainder", self.remainder, self.transformers_[-1][2])]
            )
    else:
        # all columns were consumed by the transformers
        transformers = self.transformers_
```
Thank you for questioning that!

